# The supplement operators as a module (issue #706 §4): the amendment and
# corrigendum CORE operators, bound — not redefined. A definition cannot
# change what "amendment" does; it binds its body's vocabulary to the
# operator through this module (the v1 sketch's `supplements:` section,
# made composable).
#
# The `ed`, `amd` and `cor` tokens stay RESERVED: they belong to the core
# machinery, and a module or definition may never claim them. What a body
# binds is its own spelling (A1, AC, /A1:2005) through its grammar, with
# the operator semantics carried by the core.

module: supplements.core
version: 1
title: "The amendment and corrigendum operators"
description: >-
  The core supplement operators as bindings: a supplement is a wrapper
  whose base is the document it amends, carrying its own ordinal and its
  own date. The module declares the wrapper identity and the generic
  supplement faces; the body's spellings bind through the composing
  definition's grammar and templates.

grammar:
  supplement_number: '1*digit'
  supplement_year: '4*digit'
  supplement_month: '2*digit'

identity:
  composition: wrapper-with-adopted
  adopted_field: base

hooks:
  supplement_semantics: >-
    What the operator DOES (revision-of relations, the exclude keys
    :supplement_year/:date on the wrapper) is core machinery; a body that
    needs more drops to a coded hook beside its definition.
  national_amendment: >-
    The national irregularities (BS "+A1", consolidated "+") are
    spellings bound by the composing definition, never redefinitions of
    the operator.
